{"slug":"generator","label":"GENERATOR","colour":"#FFA6A6","items":[{"slug":"john-frazer","name":"John Frazer","jobtitle":"Researcher, designer, educator","recorded":"2014-11-05","hls":"https://customer-syg1o9n270h63juf.cloudflarestream.com/46fb96d875fa8e55796776191eaa7735/manifest/video.m3u8","start":209,"end":310,"topicId":"generator","segments":[{"start":209,"end":310,"topicId":"generator"}],"cards":[{"time":213.68,"title":"Nick Bailey","id":"nick-bailey"},{"time":216.68,"title":"Cambridge University","id":"cambridge-university"},{"time":219.32,"title":"GENERATOR","id":"generator"},{"time":277.72,"title":"Tektronix 405x","id":"tektronix-405x"}]},{"slug":"john-frazer","name":"John Frazer","jobtitle":"Researcher, designer, educator","recorded":"2014-11-05","hls":"https://customer-syg1o9n270h63juf.cloudflarestream.com/46fb96d875fa8e55796776191eaa7735/manifest/video.m3u8","start":332,"end":652,"topicId":"generator","segments":[{"start":332,"end":652,"topicId":"generator"}],"cards":[{"time":340.76,"title":"Ron Herron","id":"ron-herron"},{"time":344.88,"title":"Archigram","id":"archigram"},{"time":408.52,"title":"Tektronix 405x","id":"tektronix-405x"},{"time":426.44,"title":"GENERATOR","id":"generator"},{"time":457.36,"title":"Deyan Sudjic","id":"deyan-sudjic"},{"time":460,"title":"RIBA","id":"riba"},{"time":541.52,"title":"Commodore PET","id":"commodore-pet"},{"time":593.04,"title":"BD","id":"bd"}]},{"slug":"john-frazer","name":"John Frazer","jobtitle":"Researcher, designer, educator","recorded":"2014-11-05","hls":"https://customer-syg1o9n270h63juf.cloudflarestream.com/46fb96d875fa8e55796776191eaa7735/manifest/video.m3u8","start":1708,"end":1721,"topicId":"generator","segments":[{"start":1708,"end":1721,"topicId":"generator"}],"cards":[{"time":1709.08,"title":"GENERATOR","id":"generator"}]}],"cards":{"Archigram":{"slug":"archigram","body":"Archigram was an <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Avant-garde\">avant-garde</a> architectural group formed in the 1960s - based at the <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Architectural_Association_School_of_Architecture\">Architectural Association</a>, <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/London\">London</a> - that was <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neofuturistic\">neofuturistic</a>, anti-heroic and pro-consumerist, drawing inspiration from technology in order to create a new reality that was solely expressed through hypothetical projects. The main members of the group were <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peter_Cook_(architect)\">Peter Cook</a>, <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Warren_Chalk&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\">Warren Chalk</a>, <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ron_Herron\">Ron Herron</a>, <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Dennis_Crompton&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\">Dennis Crompton</a>, <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Michael_Webb_(architect)\">Michael Webb</a> and <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=David_Greene_(architect)&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\">David Greene</a>.","subtitle":"Peter Cook, Warren Chalk, Ron Herron, Dennis Crompton, Michael Webb and David Greene","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/archigram.jpg","external":"http://designmuseum.org/design/archigram","location":"https://www.google.com/maps/place/36+Bedford+Square,+AA+School+of+Architecture,+Fitzrovia,+London+WC1B+3ES,+UK/@51.5183066,-0.1307022,17z","also":[{"slug":"bernard-tschumi","name":"Bernard Tschumi","time":770.36,"topicId":"scene","topicLabel":"Scene"},{"slug":"john-frazer","name":"John Frazer","time":344.88,"topicId":"generator","topicLabel":"GENERATOR"},{"slug":"john-frazer","name":"John Frazer","time":1125.6,"topicId":"potteries-thinkbelt","topicLabel":"POTTERIES THINKBELT"},{"slug":"john-frazer","name":"John Frazer","time":1192.04,"topicId":"potteries-thinkbelt","topicLabel":"POTTERIES THINKBELT"},{"slug":"thomas-weaver","name":"Thomas Weaver","time":644.08,"topicId":"institutions","topicLabel":"Institutions"},{"slug":"will-alsop","name":"Will Alsop","time":1356.68,"topicId":"conscience","topicLabel":"Conscience"}]},"BD":{"slug":"bd","body":"Building Design, or BD, is a weekly <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Architecture\">architectural</a> magazine and digital title in the <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Kingdom\">United Kingdom</a>. BD was launched in 1970 by publisher Morgan Grampian as a closed circulation weekly as <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/High-tech_architecture\">high-tech architecture</a> was just starting to take-off. It ceased its print edition in March 2014, remaining a digital only publication. The title soon carved a special niche for itself because of the extent of its news coverage, range of job adverts as well as its campaigning voice. Its tabloid format and news based content has earned it the reputation as one of the UK's best business titles leading to a clutch of industry awards. It is the most requested title in the library of the <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_Institute_of_British_Architects\">Royal Institute of British Architects</a>. Unlike most other architectural publications BD’s editors, with a few exceptions, are journalists not architects.","subtitle":null,"image":null,"external":null,"location":null,"also":[{"slug":"jeremy-melvin","name":"Jeremy Melvin","time":624.08,"topicId":"interaction-centre","topicLabel":"INTERACTION CENTRE"},{"slug":"john-frazer","name":"John Frazer","time":593.04,"topicId":"generator","topicLabel":"GENERATOR"},{"slug":"paul-finch","name":"Paul Finch","time":275.88,"topicId":"communications","topicLabel":"Communications"},{"slug":"paul-finch","name":"Paul Finch","time":851.52,"topicId":"network","topicLabel":"Network"},{"slug":"paul-finch","name":"Paul Finch","time":1500.24,"topicId":"communications","topicLabel":"Communications"},{"slug":"peter-murray","name":"Peter Murray","time":188.16,"topicId":"personality","topicLabel":"Personality"}]},"Cambridge University":{"slug":"cambridge-university","body":"The Department of Architecture is part of the Faculty of Architecture and History of Art in the <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/University_of_Cambridge\">University of Cambridge</a>. Both Departments are housed in Scroope Terrace on Trumpington Street and they share a library. The Faculty celebrated its Centenary in 2012. The <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/University_of_Cambridge\">University of Cambridge</a> is one of the world's leading Universities and the Architecture Department is consistently rated in the top three in national surveys.\r\n\r\nThe school has a strong research reputation in both history/theory and in scientific research, particularly into modeling and sustainable design.","subtitle":"Faculty of Architecture","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/cambridge-university.jpg","external":"http://www.arct.cam.ac.uk","location":"https://www.google.com/maps/place/Faculty+Of+Architecture+%26+History+Of+Art/@52.1984,0.121524,17z","also":[{"slug":"carlos-villanueva-brandt","name":"Carlos Villanueva Brandt","time":475.92,"topicId":null,"topicLabel":null},{"slug":"jeremy-melvin","name":"Jeremy Melvin","time":1188.32,"topicId":"potteries-thinkbelt","topicLabel":"POTTERIES THINKBELT"},{"slug":"john-frazer","name":"John Frazer","time":216.68,"topicId":"generator","topicLabel":"GENERATOR"},{"slug":"peter-murray","name":"Peter Murray","time":696.36,"topicId":"institutions","topicLabel":"Institutions"}]},"Commodore PET":{"slug":"commodore-pet","body":"<p class=\"MsoNormal\">The Commodore PET (Personal Electronic Transactor) is a <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Home_computer\">home</a>/<a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Personal_computer\">personal computer</a> produced in 1977 by <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Commodore_International\">Commodore International</a>. A top-seller in the Canadian and United States educational markets, it was Commodore's first full-featured computer, and formed the basis for their entire <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/8-bit\">8-bit</a> product line.</p>","subtitle":"Computer","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/commodore-pet.jpg","external":"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Commodore_PET","location":"https://www.google.com/maps/place/Toronto,+ON,+Canada/@43.7182713,-79.3777061,11z","also":[{"slug":"john-frazer","name":"John Frazer","time":541.52,"topicId":"generator","topicLabel":"GENERATOR"}]},"Deyan Sudjic":{"slug":"deyan-sudjic","body":"Deyan Sudjic, (b. 6 September 1952) is a writer, broadcaster and director of the <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Design_Museum\">Design Museum</a>, <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/London\">London</a>, England. He grew up in <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Acton\">Acton</a>, west London; his parents spoke <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Serbo-Croat\">Serbo-Croat</a> at home. His father worked for the <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BBC_World_Service\">BBC World Service</a>.","subtitle":"Writer, broadcaster","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/deyan-sudjic.jpg","external":"https://designmuseum.org/about-the-museum","location":"https://www.google.com/maps/place/Design+Museum/@51.502611,-0.072009,15z","also":[{"slug":"john-frazer","name":"John Frazer","time":457.36,"topicId":"generator","topicLabel":"GENERATOR"},{"slug":"peter-murray","name":"Peter Murray","time":816.96,"topicId":"institutions","topicLabel":"Institutions"}]},"GENERATOR":{"slug":"generator","body":"The Generator, an early investigation into artificially intelligent architecture, was designed with no specific program, but only a desired end-effect, in mind. The project was commissioned by Howard Gilman for a site at the Gilman Paper Corporation's White Oak Plantation in Florida to provide a facility to house dance, theater, and visiting artists. Cedric Price explored a type of architecture that, like medicine, would operate less as a remedy for the ills of society and more as a preventive system, creating flexible conditions previously thought impossible within a socially beneficial environment.","subtitle":"Faculty of Architecture","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/generator.jpg","external":"http://www.moma.org/collection/object.php?object_id=895","location":"https://www.google.com/maps/place/White+Oak,+GA+31568,+USA/@31.0313095,-81.7339961,13z","also":[{"slug":"john-frazer","name":"John Frazer","time":219.32,"topicId":"generator","topicLabel":"GENERATOR"},{"slug":"john-frazer","name":"John Frazer","time":426.44,"topicId":"generator","topicLabel":"GENERATOR"},{"slug":"john-frazer","name":"John Frazer","time":658.12,"topicId":"conscience","topicLabel":"Conscience"},{"slug":"john-frazer","name":"John Frazer","time":786.36,"topicId":"technology","topicLabel":"Technology"},{"slug":"john-frazer","name":"John Frazer","time":1683.8,"topicId":"thinking","topicLabel":"Thinking"},{"slug":"john-frazer","name":"John Frazer","time":1709.08,"topicId":"generator","topicLabel":"GENERATOR"},{"slug":"samantha-hardingham","name":"Samantha Hardingham","time":677.68,"topicId":"fun-palace","topicLabel":"FUN PALACE"},{"slug":"samantha-hardingham","name":"Samantha Hardingham","time":1272.88,"topicId":"enabling","topicLabel":"Enabling"}]},"Nick Bailey":{"slug":"nick-bailey","body":"Nick Bailey is currently the Professor of Urban Regeneration at the University of Westminster and also the Faculty Research Director of Architecture and the Built Environment.","subtitle":"Professor, Westminster University","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/nick-bailey.jpg","external":"http://www.westminster.ac.uk/about-us/our-people/directory/bailey-professor-nick","location":"https://www.google.com/maps/place/35+Marylebone+Rd,+University+of+Westminster,+Marylebone,+London+NW1+5LS,+UK/@51.5221037,-0.1543502,17z","also":[{"slug":"john-frazer","name":"John Frazer","time":213.68,"topicId":"generator","topicLabel":"GENERATOR"}]},"RIBA":{"slug":"riba","body":"The Royal Institute of British Architects (RIBA) is a <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Professional_body\">professional body</a> for architects primarily in the <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Kingdom\">United Kingdom</a>, but also internationally, founded for the advancement of architecture under its charter granted in 1837 and Supplemental Charter granted in 1971.","subtitle":"Royal Institute of British Architects","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/riba.jpg","external":"http://www.architecture.com","location":"https://maps.google.com/?q=66+Portland+Pl,+London+W1B,+UK","also":[{"slug":"john-frazer","name":"John Frazer","time":460,"topicId":"generator","topicLabel":"GENERATOR"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":625.52,"topicId":"potteries-thinkbelt","topicLabel":"POTTERIES THINKBELT"},{"slug":"thomas-weaver","name":"Thomas Weaver","time":742.16,"topicId":"appearance","topicLabel":"Appearance"}]},"Ron Herron":{"slug":"ron-herron","body":"<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Ron Herron (born in 1930 in <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/London\">London</a>, <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/England\">England</a>. Died 1994) was a notable English <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Architect\">architect</a> and teacher. He was perhaps best known for his work with the seminal English experimental architecture collective <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Archigram\">Archigram</a>, which was formed in London in the early 1960s. Herron is the creator of one of the group's best known and celebrated projects, The <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Walking_city\">Walking city</a>.</p>","subtitle":"Architect","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/ron-herron.jpg","external":"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ron_Herron","location":"https://www.google.com/maps/place/London,+UK","also":[{"slug":"john-frazer","name":"John Frazer","time":340.76,"topicId":"generator","topicLabel":"GENERATOR"}]},"Tektronix 405x":{"slug":"tektronix-405x","body":"<p class=\"MsoNormal\">The Tektronix 405x series was a series of graphics <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Microcomputer\">microcomputers</a> produced by <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tektronix\">Tektronix</a> in the late 1970s through the early 1980s. The display technology was similar to the <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tektronix_4014\">Tektronix 4014</a> terminal, using a <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Storage_tube\">storage tube</a> display to avoid the need for <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Video_RAM\">video RAM</a>. The 4051 and 4052 included a <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CPU\">CPU</a>, up to 32 <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kilobyte\">kilobytes</a> of <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/RAM\">RAM</a>, <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Computer_keyboard\">keyboard</a>, 11\" <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cathode_ray_tube\">CRT</a> and full-sized 300 kilobyte 3M DC-300 series high speed data cartridge (as opposed to the <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Compact_Cassette\">cassette</a> tape drive used by the <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HP_9830\">HP 9830</a>) to the right of the CRT in a single, desktop-sized housing. All models included a <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GPIB\">GPIB</a> parallel bus interface for controlling lab and test equipment and external input and output peripherals. A simple <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operating_system\">operating system</a> and <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BASIC\">BASIC</a> <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Interpreter_(computing)\">interpreter</a> were included in ROM. A hard copy device was available which made copies of the screen.</p>","subtitle":"Computer","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/tektronix-405x.jpg","external":"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tektronix_405x","location":"https://www.google.com/maps/place/Beaverton,+OR,+USA","also":[{"slug":"john-frazer","name":"John Frazer","time":277.72,"topicId":"generator","topicLabel":"GENERATOR"},{"slug":"john-frazer","name":"John Frazer","time":408.52,"topicId":"generator","topicLabel":"GENERATOR"}]}}}